Does glibc's dirname really modify? The glibc docs: "dirname() and basename() may modify the contents of path" — yes (the SUS variant does; GNU-specific version doesn't… glibc provides both: the GNU version with <string.h> does not modify? hmm: glibc's string.h dirname is the POSIX-compliant one that may modify; libgen.h has the same). Empirically the destruction happened (exportname became "/tmp/treecfg/cow"). So, yes, use two copies. Actually — glibc's dirname: scans for the last '/', ignores trailing slashes, then… returns a truncated pointer within the string… the empirical evidence is decisive: the client->exportname buffer was modified in place.

